# Environment variables for local-sql-agent
# Fill in your actual values. NEVER commit .env!

# OpenAI-compatible LLM endpoint (vLLM / LM Studio / llama.cpp / a local
# gpt-oss server, or OpenAI's own hosted API -- selected entirely by
# base_url). This is the trivial single-endpoint case; see LLM_ENDPOINTS/
# LLM_ROUTES below for routing across more than one endpoint.
OPENAI_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000/v1
OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-oss-20b
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# ── HTTP server binding (python -m api) ─────────────────────────────────────
# Interface/port `python -m api` (api/__main__.py) binds this API to. The
# port has always been settable on the `uvicorn` command line (`--port`) but
# NOT here -- these two exist so `.env` is a complete description of where
# the server binds, for the launcher. A plain `uvicorn api.server:app --host
# ... --port ...` invocation (the command docs/deployment-runbook.md and
# README.md document) is unaffected either way: uvicorn never reads these.
#
# API_HOST defaults to 127.0.0.1 (loopback only -- a fresh checkout is not
# reachable from the network until you widen it on purpose). The documented
# deployment command binds 0.0.0.0 explicitly on its command line instead of
# relying on a default here -- see docs/deployment-runbook.md step 4.
# API_HOST=127.0.0.1
# API_PORT=8000

# Level the application's OWN loggers (the startup provenance banner, the
# CORS-allowlist line, everything under api/ llm/ security/ session/
# database/ ...) emit at. Neither documented way of starting the API
# (plain `uvicorn api.server:app ...`, or `python -m api`) otherwise makes
# an INFO line reach anywhere: uvicorn's own default logging config only
# covers its own uvicorn.*/uvicorn.access loggers, never the root one --
# so this codebase's own INFO lines were silently dropped before this
# setting existed. Applied once, at startup, only if nothing (not an
# operator's own logging config, not this same check on a prior run in
# the same process) has already attached a handler to the root logger --
# see core/logging_setup.py.
# LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# ── SQL Server: one connection (no datasources.yaml) ──────────────────────
# DB_CONNECTION_URL is used ONLY when project_config/datasources.yaml does
# NOT exist -- the single-source shape every deployment starts with.
#
# Easiest: leave the password out of the URL and give it raw in DB_PASSWORD.
# The value is the password exactly as the database knows it -- NO URL
# encoding (write p@ss/w:rd#1, not p%40ss...). The code sets it on the parsed
# URL, so every special character is escaped for you.
# Replace username, server and YourDatabase (the start-up check refuses the
# placeholder host "username@server" until you do).
DB_CONNECTION_URL=mssql+pyodbc://username@server:1433/YourDatabase?driver=ODBC+Driver+18+for+SQL+Server&TrustServerCertificate=yes
# DB_PASSWORD=your_password_here
#
# Without DB_PASSWORD the URL is used exactly as written, so a password
# written INSIDE it must be URL-encoded by hand: "@" is %40, "/" is %2F,
# ":" is %3A, "%" is %25, "#" is %23, "?" is %3F, e.g.
#   mssql+pyodbc://username:p%40ss@server:1433/YourDatabase?driver=...
# A raw password inside the URL cannot be told apart from an encoded one
# (is "%40" the characters %40 or an "@"?), so it is never auto-detected or
# re-encoded. A password in both DB_PASSWORD and the URL is refused at
# start-up. Windows authentication: use "&trusted_connection=yes" in the URL
# with no user name and no password.

# ── Multiple warehouse data sources (optional) ──────────────────────────────
# A deployment that queries more than one database -- a second database on
# the same server, another server -- adds project_config/datasources.yaml
# (copy project_config.example/datasources.example.yaml). The YAML describes
# each connection (host, database, driver, login); .env holds only the
# secrets, one variable per source, named by password_env in the YAML.
# DB_CONNECTION_URL and DB_PASSWORD above are then unused. See
# docs/design/DATASOURCES.md.
#
# The value is the RAW password: no URL encoding, no quotes around it
# (unless your .env syntax needs them, e.g. a value containing " #"). The
# code builds and escapes the URL itself.
#
# DB_PASSWORD_SALES=your_password_here
# DB_PASSWORD_INVENTORY=your_password_here
#
# A login stored in the environment instead of the YAML (username_env):
# DB_USER_FINANCE=nlq_reader
#
# Sources written against 6.1/6.2 with url_env still work. Their variable
# holds a complete SQLAlchemy URL, whose password must be URL-encoded:
# DB_URL_WAREHOUSE2=mssql+pyodbc://nlq_reader:p%40ss@10.0.0.6:1433/DB?driver=ODBC+Driver+18+for+SQL+Server

# Client application name identified to the warehouse when the connection
# is mssql+pyodbc (a no-op for sqlite/other backends, and never overrides a
# name the URL already sets) -- lets a DBA attribute this app's sessions in
# sys.dm_exec_sessions/sys.dm_exec_requests or a trace instead of seeing an
# unlabelled connection. See database/connection_identity.py.
DB_APPLICATION_NAME=local-sql-agent

# Whether database.connection.get_engine (and, for a non-SQLite APP_DB_URL,
# appdb.engine.build_engine) pings a pooled connection on checkout at all.
# Default true, but the probe is now IDLE-AWARE (see DB_POOL_PING_IDLE_SECONDS
# below): only a connection that has actually sat unused for at least that
# many seconds is probed with a "SELECT 1" (a DBA-visible round trip); one
# reused sooner is handed over unprobed. This is what keeps a stale
# connection (the SQL Server side closed it, a firewall/load-balancer
# idle-timed it out) from silently failing a real query, without paying a
# probe on every single checkout regardless of how recently it was used.
# Turning this off removes the probe entirely, at every idle threshold; a
# stale connection then fails its first real query once and is
# transparently retried on a fresh one -- a reasonable trade only once
# DB_POOL_RECYCLE_SECONDS (below) is set comfortably under whatever idle
# timeout the network path to the warehouse (SQL Server itself, a
# firewall, a load balancer) actually enforces. See
# config.Settings.db_pool_pre_ping and docs/deployment-runbook.md for the
# full trade-off.
DB_POOL_PRE_PING=true

# How long (seconds) a pooled connection must have sat unused before
# DB_POOL_PRE_PING=true actually probes it on checkout, rather than handing
# it straight to the caller. Default 60. Set to 0 to ping on EVERY checkout
# regardless of idle time -- the old, simpler pool_pre_ping=True behaviour,
# useful if you want that guarantee back rather than the idle-aware
# trade-off. Has no effect when DB_POOL_PRE_PING=false. See
# config.Settings.db_pool_ping_idle_seconds and
# database/pool_ping.py for the mechanism.
DB_POOL_PING_IDLE_SECONDS=60

# Seconds after which a pooled connection is recycled (SQLAlchemy's
# pool_recycle), previously a hardcoded 3600. Lowering this below the
# warehouse/network path's real idle timeout is the documented
# alternative to leaving DB_POOL_PRE_PING on -- see above.
DB_POOL_RECYCLE_SECONDS=3600

# ── Runtime behaviour ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set to 1/true/yes to run tests/integration/test_executor_live.py against
# THIS DB_CONNECTION_URL (a real database). Left unset, those tests are
# skipped cleanly -- see that file's module docstring. Not read by the
# application itself, only by that opt-in test suite.
# RUN_LIVE_DB_TESTS=1

QUERY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60
MAX_ROWS_RETURNED=1000
# Row cap injected into generated SQL (as TOP n) when it has no row-limit
# clause of its own. Optional — defaults to MAX_ROWS_RETURNED if unset.
# DEFAULT_TOP_N=1000
LOG_DIR=logs
EXPORT_DIR=exports
CACHE_TTL_SECONDS=300
CACHE_MAX_SIZE=256

# How long (seconds) GET /health reuses its last database-ping result
# before probing the warehouse again, so frequent external monitors do
# not each cause their own round trip. 0 disables caching (every call
# pings live).
HEALTH_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS=15

# How long (seconds) the admin panel's warehouse-touching cards (deployment
# checks, schema drift) reuse their last result before re-running -- the
# panel's own per-card refresh button (?refresh=1) bypasses a still-valid
# cached value for that one request without changing this setting. 0
# disables caching (every request runs the checks live). See
# api/admin_result_cache.py and docs/deployment-runbook.md.
ADMIN_EXPENSIVE_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS=300

# Rate limiting (API). Bucketed per (authenticated principal, IP) pair --
# see api/middleware.py's RateLimitMiddleware docstring -- so in the
# common deployment shape (one web UI, one shared service key, many
# analysts behind it) every analyst using that UI still shares ONE
# bucket. 600/60s = 10 requests/sec sustained, sized for roughly 30
# analysts each firing an interactive query (a human clicking "ask"
# every few seconds, not a batch client) at once: 30 x one request per
# 3s = 600/min. Raise this if legitimate use is getting 429s (more
# analysts, or a UI that fires several requests per interaction); lower
# it for a deployment with only a handful of analysts that wants a
# tighter ceiling on a misbehaving client or scripting mistake. See
# config.Settings.rate_limit_requests for the full reasoning.
RATE_LIMIT_REQUESTS=600       # requests allowed per window, PER (principal, ip) bucket
RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SEC=60      # window size in seconds
RATE_LIMIT_BURST=40           # extra burst tokens above sustained rate -- absorbs one
                               # page load's worth of parallel calls without eating
                               # into the sustained rate; raise/lower alongside REQUESTS
MAX_CONCURRENT_REQUESTS=10   # max parallel /query requests before 503

# Comma-separated IPs of reverse proxies allowed to supply X-Forwarded-For
# / X-Real-IP for rate-limiting purposes. Leave empty (default) unless this
# server sits behind a known proxy — otherwise a direct client can bypass
# its own per-IP rate limit by sending a different made-up
# X-Forwarded-For value on every request.
# Example: TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS=10.0.0.5,10.0.0.6
TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS=

# Max distinct client IP buckets the rate limiter keeps in memory at once.
# Beyond this, the least-recently-seen bucket is evicted to make room for
# a new one, bounding memory use against a flood of distinct/spoofed IPs.
RATE_LIMIT_MAX_TRACKED_IPS=10000

# Schema auto-discovery
# Set AUTO_DISCOVER_SCHEMA=true to inspect the live database on startup
# and build relationship hints automatically (requires DB_CONNECTION_URL).
# If project_config/relationships.yaml exists, that file is used instead.
AUTO_DISCOVER_SCHEMA=false
SCHEMA_CACHE_TTL=3600         # seconds to cache discovered schema (0 = no cache)

# Small-dimension value vocabulary (retrieval/dimension_vocabulary.py) --
# lets a question name a hall/currency/broker/etc. by its actual stored
# value, not just a hand-maintained alias. Cold/stale entries self-heal via
# a background refresh from the request path itself either way; the two
# flags below only change when the FIRST fetch of each dimension happens,
# and how strict the in-question match is.
#
# On by default: pre-warms every prefetched dimension's value list at
# startup instead of leaving each dimension's first-ever fetch to whichever
# request happens to mention it first (which otherwise silently answers
# that one question as if the value had not been named at all). A failed
# warm-up is logged and does not stop the server from starting.
DIMENSION_VOCABULARY_WARM_ON_STARTUP=true
# On by default: lets a question name only PART of a stored value (e.g.
# the distinguishing word of a hall name without repeating the generic
# word every one of that dimension's values shares) instead of requiring
# the whole value verbatim. See retrieval/dimension_vocabulary.py's module
# docstring, "Matching rules", for the scoring and what turning this off
# trades away.
DIMENSION_VOCABULARY_TOKEN_FALLBACK_ENABLED=true

# Setup wizard LLM settings (optional overrides for the setup wizard only)
WIZARD_LLM_PROVIDER=openai    # openai | mock
WIZARD_LLM_MODEL=gpt-oss-20b  # model name (overrides per-provider model)
WIZARD_LLM_BASE_URL=          # optional: custom OpenAI-compatible endpoint
WIZARD_LANGUAGE=fa            # default language for alias generation (fa | en | both)

# Production engine LLM router (llm/router.py). Which backend answers
# every task (SQL generation, interpretation, assumption extraction) by
# default. "openai" (the default) routes through llm/endpoints.py -- see
# LLM_ENDPOINTS/LLM_ROUTES below for anything beyond the trivial
# single-endpoint case, and LLM_ALLOW_REMOTE below for the governance gate.
LLM_PROVIDER=openai            # openai | mock

# Sending schema, business-rule text, or query-result rows to an untrusted
# endpoint is NOT enabled just by setting OPENAI_BASE_URL/LLM_ENDPOINTS
# above -- this product's premise is "runs on your infrastructure". Set
# this to true only as a deliberate, per-deployment opt-in; every call to
# an untrusted endpoint made while it is true is written to the audit trail.
LLM_ALLOW_REMOTE=false

# Explicit trust override for the single "default" endpoint (OPENAI_BASE_URL
# above). Unset (default) auto-detects: loopback/private/*.local addresses
# are trusted, a public hostname (e.g. api.openai.com) is not. Set
# true/false to override that heuristic for this specific deployment.
# LLM_TRUSTED=true

# Additional named endpoints beyond the trivial "default" one, as a JSON
# array -- each entry needs at least name/base_url/model ("api_key" and
# "trusted" are optional). Pair with LLM_ROUTES below to route specific
# tasks to specific endpoints with a fallback chain. Example:
# LLM_ENDPOINTS=[{"name":"local","base_url":"http://localhost:8000/v1","model":"gpt-oss-20b"},{"name":"hosted","base_url":"https://api.openai.com/v1","model":"gpt-4o-mini","api_key":"sk-..."}]
# LLM_ROUTES={"sql_generation":["local","hosted"],"interpretation":["local"]}

# Optional per-task latency budget (seconds), applied uniformly to every
# TaskType by LLMRouter.from_settings(). Unset (default) disables it.
# LLM_TASK_BUDGET_SECONDS=30

# Phase 2 task 3 feature flag: ask for one constrained JSON object
# ({"sql", "out_of_scope", "confidence", "assumptions"}) instead of free
# text + clean_sql regex surgery. Off until the golden-set evaluation
# (python -m eval.cli run --live --structured) justifies flipping it --
# see the Phase 2 report for whether that evaluation has been run yet.
LLM_STRUCTURED_OUTPUT=false

# ── Completion length, and reasoning models ────────────────────────────────
# Max tokens the model may generate per request (sent as max_tokens).
# Defaults to 512.
#
# 512 is generous for a SQL statement, which is a few dozen tokens. It is
# NOT generous for a REASONING model -- Qwen3, DeepSeek-R1, gpt-oss and
# similar spend their completion budget thinking BEFORE they answer. Such a
# model can consume all 512 tokens on reasoning and be cut off before
# emitting a single character of SQL.
#
# What that looks like, because it does not look like a token limit:
#   - the UI says the model returned nothing (EMPTY_SQL_RESPONSE)
#   - the audit record shows finish_reason="length",
#     completion_tokens exactly equal to this value,
#     reasoning_detected=true, and generated_sql=""
#   - it is slow, because the correction loop retries the whole thing
#
# If you see that combination, this setting is the cause. Either raise it
# (2048-4096 is a reasonable range for a reasoning model), or turn the
# model's reasoning off with LLM_EXTRA_BODY below -- which is faster and
# cheaper, since reasoning tokens are billed and waited on either way.
# LLM_NUM_PREDICT=512

# Extra JSON fields merged into every chat-completions request body.
# Empty (the default) sends exactly what this project has always sent.
#
# This exists because "turn the model's reasoning off" is not part of the
# OpenAI chat-completions schema, and every inference server spells it
# differently. Rather than this project guessing at each server's private
# vocabulary -- and silently sending the wrong key when it guessed wrong --
# these fields are passed through verbatim. They are YOUR server's fields.
#
# Turning Qwen3's thinking off, on vLLM or SGLang:
# LLM_EXTRA_BODY={"chat_template_kwargs":{"enable_thinking":false}}
#
# Ollama:
# LLM_EXTRA_BODY={"think":false}
#
# An OpenAI reasoning model, minimising rather than disabling:
# LLM_EXTRA_BODY={"reasoning_effort":"low"}
#
# Check your server's own documentation -- a field it does not recognise is
# usually ignored silently, so verify the effect in the audit record
# (reasoning_detected should become false, completion_tokens should drop)
# rather than assuming it took.
#
# Must be a JSON OBJECT. It may not set model, messages, temperature,
# top_p, seed, max_tokens, stop, stream or n: those come from their own
# settings and are reported in the llm status block, so overriding them
# here would make that block describe a request that was never sent. A
# malformed value fails at start-up, not on the first question.
# LLM_EXTRA_BODY=

# JSONL log rotation (logs/logger.py::save_log, observability/audit.py)
# Applies to both query_log.jsonl (REPL) and audit_log.jsonl (API audit
# trail -- the ONLY source of real accuracy/latency numbers a first
# production deployment will ever produce; losing its earliest records to
# a rotation nobody was watching is silent and unrecoverable). Read
# through config.py's Settings (log_max_bytes / log_backup_count) at call
# time, like every other setting.
# Size cap per log file, in bytes, before it is rotated (query_log.jsonl ->
# query_log.jsonl.1, etc). <= 0 disables rotation (unbounded growth).
# 50 MiB x 20 backups (below) = ~1 GiB / ~1M records retained at this
# project's observed ~1 KB/record average -- raise LOG_MAX_BYTES if real
# traffic runs materially larger per record than that; raise
# LOG_BACKUP_COUNT if a deployment's total volume could plausibly exceed
# ~1M records before someone archives the file. Lower either only if disk
# space is a tighter constraint than audit history, which for a
# compliance/analysis log is rarely the right trade.
LOG_MAX_BYTES=52428800
# Number of rotated backups to retain. The oldest beyond this count is
# deleted on each rotation. <= 0 keeps no history (file is cleared instead
# of shifted to .1 on rotation).
LOG_BACKUP_COUNT=20

# ── Evaluation harness regression gate (eval/baseline.py) ────────────────
# What counts as a regression when `python -m eval.cli run --baseline ...`
# compares a new golden-set run against a saved one. Tuning, not a fixed
# technical fact -- how much accuracy/latency variance is tolerable is a
# property of THIS deployment's hardware and traffic. Each has its own
# `python -m eval.cli run --max-...` flag that overrides these per
# invocation without touching the environment.
#
# Percentage-point drop in accuracy (baseline minus current) above which a
# run is flagged. Raise this to tolerate noisier golden-set accuracy;
# lower it to catch smaller regressions sooner.
EVAL_MAX_ACCURACY_DROP_PCT=5.0
# Relative percentage increase in p95 latency (vs baseline) above which a
# run is flagged. Raise this on slower or more variable hardware where
# some latency drift run-to-run is normal; lower it on fast, dedicated
# hardware where a latency regression should stand out immediately.
EVAL_MAX_LATENCY_P95_INCREASE_PCT=20.0
# Absolute increase in guard-rejected cases (vs baseline) above which a
# run is flagged. Defaults to 0 -- any new guard rejection is safety-
# relevant (generated SQL that used to pass the security guard no longer
# does). Raise only if you have a specific, understood reason more guard
# rejections are expected.
EVAL_MAX_GUARD_REJECTION_INCREASE=0

# ── Phase 8: API-key authentication ─────────────────────────────────────
# Named API keys, not JWT/OIDC -- see docs/api-contract-v2.md's
# authentication section for the rationale. Fail-closed by default: if
# AUTH_REQUIRED=true (the default) and neither API_KEYS_FILE nor
# API_KEYS_JSON has a configured key, the server refuses to start rather
# than run with a front door nobody can open.
#
# Issue a key with: python -m scripts.issue_api_key --id analyst-1 --name "Jane Analyst"
# It prints the raw key ONCE (copy it -- it is not recoverable afterwards)
# and the entry to add to the key array below. Only the SHA-256 hex digest
# of a key is ever stored here -- never the raw key itself. The raw key is
# what a person pastes into the browser; the digest is what goes here.
#
# API_KEYS_JSON=[{"id":"analyst-1","name":"Jane Analyst","key_sha256":"<64-hex-char digest>"}]
#
# Admin capabilities are granted at issue time by flag, not by hand-editing
# this array: --admin, --operations, --security, or --full-admin for all
# three (docs/admin-panel-architecture.md SS2). The admin panel needs
# 'admin' AND at least one of the other two -- 'admin' alone loads a panel
# where six of its ten sections return 403. At least one key must carry
# them: the first admin of each kind comes from here, never from a web flow.
#
# API_KEYS_JSON=[{"id":"admin-1","name":"Admin","key_sha256":"<64-hex-char digest>","admin":true,"operations":true,"security":true}]
#
# One line works as it is. Writing this across several lines requires
# wrapping the WHOLE value in single quotes -- .env is line-based, so without
# them dotenv takes "[" as the entire value and reads the remaining lines as
# new keys -- and the value must then contain no apostrophe (a name such as
# "Ali's key" breaks it). Double quotes around the array break the JSON. The
# server refuses to start on such a line and names it; for more than one key,
# use API_KEYS_FILE below instead.
API_KEYS_JSON=

# RECOMMENDED for more than one key: put the same JSON array in a file and
# name it here. No .env quoting rules apply, so it can be pretty-printed and
# can contain apostrophes. A relative path is resolved against the repository
# root (not the working directory), like PROJECT_CONFIG_DIR. Use
# project_config/api_keys.json -- project_config/ is git-ignored, so key
# digests stay out of version control. Start from the template: copy
# project_config.example/api_keys.example.json to project_config/api_keys.json,
# then replace each key_sha256 with the digest scripts/issue_api_key.py prints
# (the server refuses to start while a placeholder is left in). Set this OR
# API_KEYS_JSON, not both
# (the server refuses to start). The file is read once at start-up: restart
# after editing it. File content, e.g. project_config/api_keys.json:
#
#   [
#     {
#       "id": "analyst-1",
#       "name": "Jane Analyst",
#       "key_sha256": "<64-hex-char digest>"
#     },
#     {
#       "id": "admin-1",
#       "name": "Admin",
#       "key_sha256": "<64-hex-char digest>",
#       "admin": true,
#       "operations": true,
#       "security": true
#     }
#   ]
#
# API_KEYS_FILE=project_config/api_keys.json
API_KEYS_FILE=

# Whether every route except GET /health requires a valid API key.
# Defaults to true (fail-closed). Setting this to false is a deliberate
# escape hatch (local development, or a deployment that already sits
# behind its own perimeter auth) -- the server logs a WARNING on every
# startup while it is false, not just the first, so a disabled front door
# is never quiet in the logs. Do not set this to false in production.
AUTH_REQUIRED=true

# Whether /docs, /redoc, and /openapi.json are served without credentials.
# Defaults to false: the generated API documentation describes exactly
# what an authenticated caller can do to production data, so it requires
# the same authentication as every other protected route by default.
APP_DOCS_PUBLIC=false

# ── Cross-origin access for the bundled web UI ─────────────────────────────
# The layout docs/fa/getting-started.md describes puts the API on one port
# and the static UI (web/) on another, which makes every call from the
# browser cross-origin. Defaults to http://localhost:8080 and
# http://127.0.0.1:8080 -- the origins that UI is documented to be served
# from -- so the local setup works without touching this.
#
# Set it when the UI is served from anywhere else. Setting it REPLACES the
# default list rather than adding to it, so a real deployment naming its own
# origin does not silently keep localhost allowed as well.
#
# Getting this wrong looks like a dead backend, not a config error: the
# browser reports a refused preflight to the page as "Failed to fetch", the
# same thing it says when nothing is listening at all. The server logs its
# effective CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS list once at startup (INFO level) precisely
# so this is checkable without guessing -- see docs/deployment-runbook.md.
#
# Worked example: API on 172.16.101.42:8076, static UI (web/, which also
# serves web/admin/) on 172.16.101.42:8077 -- two different ports on the
# same host still counts as a different origin to the browser:
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://172.16.101.42:8077
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://your-ui-host:8080

# ── Target SQL dialect ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# tsql (default) and sqlite are verified by execution; postgres and mysql
# transpile and re-validate but are unverified. Must match the backend of
# every configured connection -- DB_CONNECTION_URL, or every source in
# datasources.yaml when that file exists -- the server refuses to start
# if any of them disagree. One dialect for the whole deployment; mixing
# database types across sources is not supported yet.
# SQL_DIALECT=tsql

# ── Conversation persistence ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# SQLite file holding the conversation index, turns and pinned preferences.
# Contains questions, generated SQL and result COLUMN NAMES -- never result
# rows. Set to an empty value to disable persistence entirely.
# SESSION_STORE_PATH=logs/sessions.db
# SESSION_RETENTION_DAYS=30
